  {
    "episode_id": 74506,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/4/74506.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 12,
    "turn_text": "yeah so of course the FAA is the federal aviation administration and that's the agency that is in charge of all things airplane and aviation and we've been working on this bill for about two years now in fact really over that just trying to lay the groundwork for in this bill passed the House of Representatives last summer in the month of June by a vote of 351 to 69 and the reason I say that Brandon is because Look, everything in Congress and Washington right now is chaotic and lots of fighting and everything else. But in a situation like this, they have a pretty consequential bill passed by such a strong bipartisan margin is really a testament to the groundwork that we laid to all the months and months we spent working on this and making sure we were doing it right. And so the bill consists of a number of different components and I'll run through a few of them. And really the main one is that We spent a lot of time thinking about the passenger experience, really trying to look at it through the eyes of those young parents traveling with their two young kids that go drive to some parking lot, then take a shuttle to the airport and then go check in, throw in the bags and trying to bring those along, check in at the airport, trying to go through security, buying your $10 bottle of water, and then ultimately getting in line, getting on the plane and going through that whole cattle call, ultimately landing at your airport, and maybe circling a few times while you're in the air, getting off the plane, waiting on your new bags, trying to get transportation out of that next airport, and really thinking about that entire curb-to-curb experience. Because what happens is you may deal with 10 different vendors, whether it's somebody doing security, somebody doing the concessions, somebody driving the shuttle, somebody checking in at the airport, all these different entities, and then behind the scenes entities, like air traffic control and others, that people don't really think about. So again, that entire experience, so everything from how do we bring to the forefront, newer technology that's going to better share, communicate with the flying public about your flight is actually going to be delayed 30 minutes. And you know that five hours ahead of time to where you don't go drive and sit at the airport for 30 minutes or as we've all done five hours, trying to use better technology to more efficiently manage the air traffic system. trying to make sure that People have more of a seamless experience through all these different entities or vendors, but I can't say enough, all about the passenger experience, all about better consumer protection at the end of the day as well. We have a big title on new technology and aviation, everything from drones and thinking about drone delivery to reconnaissance type activities for security, safety, for looking after a hurricane or a disaster and identifying people who may need help. and hazardous conditions, advanced air mobility, which is new technology that ultimately would be picking up passengers to fly them like an uber today. And then something I know you care a lot about personally is general aviation. And so what it does is really tries to it has an entire title on general aviation, which of course is smaller airports, private pilots, things like that, which is the first time ever. But trying to think about how to better incorporate that group of the flying public into the national airspace system, into air traffic control, and in a way that's better, that's more efficient. So really excited about this legislation, and it should ultimately be signed into law by the president in the next few days.",
