  {
    "episode_id": 74506,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/4/74506.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 44,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, Brandon, just to level set things a little bit back about three and a half years ago, lowest gasoline prices in Louisiana. We're about $1.74 a gallon, right? Three and a half years ago, $1.74 a gallon. I'm driving around this weekend. I saw prices in excess of twice that, average prices probably this weekend. And I saw we're somewhere probably around 3.15 or something like that, a gallon. And I'll say it again, lowest prices three and a half years ago were $1.74 a gallon. And this is really having a huge impact on people's ability to just afford to live. But because it's not just about filling your car, your truck, it's not just about paying your utility bill. It's the cost that, what's your grocery stores? They're paying more because it costs them more to have their deliveries. Amazon, they're paying more because it's costing them more to have things delivered to them and then delivered to you. So it's one of the things that really replicates or cascades through the economy more than anything else. So it contributes to higher grocery prices. It contributes to higher prices online. No matter what it is that you're buying, it contributes to higher services, costs that we're paying, and even higher airline prices, Uber prices, things along those lines. What we're seeing, and this is something that's not really thought about a lot, but right now Russia has been profiting and profiting because they have a lot of energy, and the higher energy prices is allowing Russia to make more money by some estimates over a hundred billion dollars in additional profits that they're then using to turn around and fund the Ukraine war. Similarly in Iran, an estimate recently said that Iran's profit, an additional profit, to the tune of about $65 billion. Again, they're using those funds to come in and attack Israel and fund these terrorist groups like Hamas and Hezbollah and Houthis and others. And then what you just saw a few weeks ago is Congress is funding the other side of the wars, giving funds to Ukraine, giving funds to Israel. So we're funding both sides of the war through bad energy policy and through appropriations. So what we need to be doing is increasing production, in fact, off the coast of Louisiana, for example, it has some of the cleanest energy sources in the world in terms of emissions intensity or carbon intensity of oil and gas. not going to Iran, not going to Russia, Venezuela and other countries that are going to then use the proceeds to attack the United States or our interests or allies around the world. And that's something we've been trying to do. We actually had a bill pass out of the Natural Resources Committee on a bipartisan basis. It was our bill called the Bridge Act, which resumes new production in the Gulf of Mexico. And I'll say it again. Recognizing that even the Biden administration says there's going to be an increase in demand for things like natural gas and gasoline. And so if you know that there's going to be a global increase up to 50% over the next 26 years, then my gosh, let's produce it where it's cleaner, where it's safer, where it's going to benefit us economically. And that's in the Gulf of Mexico. So our bill does that and really needs to be a part of the energy solution moving forward.",
