  {
    "episode_id": 74506,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/4/74506.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 51,
    "turn_text": "Things like wind and solar are known as intermittent power. The wind isn't always blowing, the sun's not always out. A lot of times when you have your greatest power intensity or needs in the house, those are the times when wind and solar are not at their peak and you end up with this thing where you're just missing. In the future, things like energy storage, battery technology may get better, but that whole base load power, whether it's natural gas, nuclear, what have you, it is absolutely critical to allow for the renewable energy to work, but that renewable energy is only going to play a small role. Look, we're going to need all of it moving forward, but we've got to make sure we're not going to force technologies that aren't ready for prime time and a way to where you're seeing in California. And even in recent years in Texas, where the reliability or the performance of those energy sources are not up to par, especially in times when you really needed a cold winter storm or a long heat wave that we may be experiencing.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_00",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "Wind and solar are classified as intermittent power sources.",
        "confidence": 0.97
      },
      {
        "text": "The availability of wind and solar energy varies over time and does not consistently align with peak household electricity demand.",
        "confidence": 0.95
